// Library upgrade coverage: the numbered scripts in a library's Upgrades folder and the operators that record, read, apply and
// delete them. This is the machinery behind the riskiest thing a Dataphor installation does - taking a new version of an
// installed application. LibraryCoverage covers creating, renaming, dropping, registering and unregistering a library;
// LibraryAdministration covers settings, files, detaching and the errors an administrator meets; LibraryDependencies covers the
// requisite tables. None of that is repeated here.
//
// Two version numbers matter, and the script keeps them apart throughout:
//   the DESCRIPTOR version - the Version column of the Libraries catalog table, read from the library's .d4l file - is what the
//     files on disk claim, and it is the CEILING of an upgrade run;
//   the RECORDED version - the Version column of the LibraryVersions catalog table, which LibraryView exposes as LoadedVersion
//     - is the version the catalog believes is applied, and it is the FLOOR.
// UpgradeLibrary(name) upgrades the library's requisites first, then runs every Upgrades/Upgrade.*.d4 of the library whose
// version is above the floor and not above the ceiling, in version order, each in its own transaction on a session of its own
// owned by the library, and records the descriptor version once it finishes. UpgradeLibraries() does that for every registered
// library. LibraryView.UpgradeRequired is simply descriptor > recorded.
//
// THE ONE ARTIFICIAL STEP. The state an upgrade runs in - registered, descriptor ahead of recorded - is the state a customer's
// server is in once the new library files have been copied in and the server restarted (LibraryUtility.LoadLibrary reads the
// recorded version before loading and writes the same value back afterwards, deliberately, so that a restart does not swallow a
// pending upgrade). A coverage script cannot restart its server, so each section below puts the catalog into that state directly
// with
//     update LibraryVersions set { Version := ... } where LibraryName = '...';
// which stands for "the files of a newer version have arrived and the server has been restarted on them". Nothing else here is
// contrived. In particular InjectUpgrade cannot be used to produce a pending upgrade: it is the operator a developer uses to
// record a change already made on their own server, so the descriptor bump it makes carries the recorded version with it
// (SystemSetLibraryDescriptorNode.ChangeLibraryVersion calls SetCurrentLibraryVersion for a registered library) and leaves
// nothing pending.
//
// WHY EACH SECTION'S TRACE IS READ IN A BATCH OF ITS OWN. What an upgrade script did is read from a table it creates in the
// library it upgrades, named here by its rooted name - .UpgradeOrder.OrderTrace. A rooted name resolves against the whole
// catalog rather than through the current library's name resolution path, so no SetLibrary is needed; but a script is executed
// one top-level statement at a time and each is compiled only when it is reached, so the statement that reads the table cannot
// be the statement that created it. Hence the shape of each section: a block that does the work, then a bare assertion.
//
// An error raised inside a plan arrives wrapped in RuntimeException 104229, so E.Code is always 104229 and never the schema
// code. The assertions below read E.GetDetailedErrorDescription(), which flattens the whole chain and prints each link as
// "<severity>:<code> ---> <message>", and look for ":<code> --->" rather than for the digits alone: the same text carries
// source paths and ":line NNN" fragments, which digits alone could match. Where a message names a library they do not assert
// the quoting of the name, because some of these messages carry it rooted, with a leading dot.
//
// Every library here is one the script creates, and each section drops what it created, which also clears its LibraryVersions
// row. The script opens no explicit transaction, and must not: library DDL is not transactional, and DropLibrary refuses inside
// a transaction the session opened itself, because dropping a library deletes its directory and a rollback cannot restore it
// (the "Library DDL is not transactional" section of docs/architecture/libraries-and-class-loading.md). That refusal reaches an
// upgrade script too, which runs inside the transaction the upgrade machinery opened for it: no upgrade script here drops a
// library, and none may. Its manifest entry turns implicit transactions off, and that is not optional
// - the upgrade runs on a session of its own, and an implicit transaction around this script would hold the catalog store's
// write lock while that session waited for it and gave up.

// Version arithmetic, and that nothing is applied twice. InjectUpgrade bumps the descriptor's revision by one, writes the
// script as Upgrades/Upgrade.<major>.<minor>.<revision>.d4 and returns that version; UpgradeVersions lists what the folder
// holds and LoadUpgrade reads one back.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeArith', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeArith');

	// Registering a library records its descriptor version as the applied version, so nothing is pending on a fresh register.
	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeArith')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) then
		raise Error('Registering a library did not record its descriptor version in LibraryVersions.');

	var LScript : String := "create table ArithTrace { ID : Integer, Trace : String, key { ID } }; insert table { row { 0 ID, 'arith;' Trace } } into ArithTrace;";
	var LInjected : VersionNumber := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeArith', LScript);
	if LInjected <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('InjectUpgrade did not return the next revision: ' + LInjected.ToString());

	if (Version from row from (Libraries where Name = 'UpgradeArith')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('InjectUpgrade did not advance the descriptor version.');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeArith')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('InjectUpgrade did not carry the recorded version along with the descriptor version.');

	if (Count from row from (UpgradeVersions('UpgradeArith') group add { Count() Count })) <> 1 then
		raise Error('UpgradeVersions did not list exactly the one injected upgrade.');

	if not exists (UpgradeVersions('UpgradeArith') where Version = VersionNumber(1, 0, 1)) then
		raise Error('UpgradeVersions did not list the injected upgrade at the version InjectUpgrade returned.');

	if LoadUpgrade('UpgradeArith', VersionNumber(1, 0, 1)) <> LScript then
		raise Error('LoadUpgrade did not return the text InjectUpgrade wrote.');

	// Stands for: the files of version 1.0.1 have arrived and the server has been restarted on them.
	update LibraryVersions set { Version := VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) } where LibraryName = 'UpgradeArith';

	if (LoadedVersion from row from (LibraryView where Name = 'UpgradeArith')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) then
		raise Error('LibraryView did not report the recorded version as the loaded version.');

	if not (UpgradeRequired from row from (LibraryView where Name = 'UpgradeArith')) then
		raise Error('LibraryView did not report an upgrade as required with the descriptor ahead of the recorded version.');

	UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeArith');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeArith')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('UpgradeLibrary did not record the descriptor version once it had finished.');

	if (UpgradeRequired from row from (LibraryView where Name = 'UpgradeArith')) then
		raise Error('LibraryView still reported an upgrade as required after one had been applied.');

	if not exists (CatalogObjects where Name = 'UpgradeArith.ArithTrace') then
		raise Error('The upgrade script did not create its table.');

	// Neither operator re-runs an upgrade already applied: both compare the descriptor version with the recorded one first. A
	// second run of this upgrade would fail on its create table, so the silence of these two calls is part of the assertion.
	UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeArith');
	UpgradeLibraries();

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeArith')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('A second upgrade run changed the recorded version.');
end;

// The row the upgrade inserted, read in a batch of its own - see the note on batches at the head of the file.
if (Trace from row from .UpgradeArith.ArithTrace) <> 'arith;' then
	raise Error('The upgrade script did not leave its row, or left it twice: ' + (Trace from row from .UpgradeArith.ArithTrace));

begin
	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeArith');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeArith');

	// DropLibrary clears the library's LibraryVersions row.
	if exists (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeArith') then
		raise Error('DropLibrary left the library version behind.');
end;

// Upgrade version numbers the engine refuses. UpgradeUtility.CheckValidVersionNumber requires a major, a minor and a revision,
// and no build, so a library whose version stops at the minor cannot have upgrades tracked for it at all.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeNoRevision', VersionNumber(1, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));

	var LCaught : String := '';
	try
		var LInjected : VersionNumber := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeNoRevision', 'begin end;');
	except on E : Error do
		LCaught := E.GetDetailedErrorDescription();
	end;
	if (IndexOf(LCaught, ':113228 --->') < 0) or (IndexOf(LCaught, 'UpgradeNoRevision') < 0) or (IndexOf(LCaught, 'because the version number "1.0.*" does not have Revision specified.') < 0) then
		raise Error('Injecting an upgrade into a library whose version has no revision did not raise 113228: ' + LCaught);

	// The refusal is raised before anything is written, so neither the folder nor the descriptor moved.
	if exists (UpgradeVersions('UpgradeNoRevision')) then
		raise Error('A refused InjectUpgrade wrote an upgrade file anyway.');

	if (Version from row from (Libraries where Name = 'UpgradeNoRevision')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0) then
		raise Error('A refused InjectUpgrade advanced the descriptor version anyway.');

	DropLibrary('UpgradeNoRevision');
end;

// The other refusal, and the one arm of SaveUpgrade that is an inject. SaveUpgrade is given the version outright, so it is
// where a version carrying a build is caught; and given no version at all it hands over to InjectUpgrade, which is how the
// IDE's upgrade editor injects. This library is deliberately left unregistered: nothing here needs it registered, and an
// unregistered library has no recorded version to keep in step.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeBadVersion', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));

	var LCaught : String := '';
	try
		SaveUpgrade('UpgradeBadVersion', VersionNumber(1, 0, 1, 1), 'begin end;');
	except on E : Error do
		LCaught := E.GetDetailedErrorDescription();
	end;
	if (IndexOf(LCaught, ':113227 --->') < 0) or (IndexOf(LCaught, 'Upgrade version number "1.0.1.1" must have Major, Minor and Revision specified, and must not have Build specified.') < 0) then
		raise Error('Saving an upgrade at a version that carries a build did not raise 113227: ' + LCaught);

	var LScript : String := 'begin end;';
	SaveUpgrade('UpgradeBadVersion', nil as VersionNumber, LScript);

	if (Version from row from (Libraries where Name = 'UpgradeBadVersion')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('SaveUpgrade with no version did not advance the descriptor version the way an inject does.');

	if LoadUpgrade('UpgradeBadVersion', VersionNumber(1, 0, 1)) <> LScript then
		raise Error('SaveUpgrade with no version did not write the script at the next revision.');

	DropLibrary('UpgradeBadVersion');
end;

// UpgradeLibrary resolves the loaded library before anything else, so a library that exists but is not registered cannot be
// upgraded. That is a plausible administrator mistake, and this is the message it produces.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeUnregistered', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));

	var LCaught : String := '';
	try
		UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeUnregistered');
	except on E : Error do
		LCaught := E.GetDetailedErrorDescription();
	end;
	if (IndexOf(LCaught, ':113242 --->') < 0) or (IndexOf(LCaught, 'UpgradeUnregistered') < 0) or (IndexOf(LCaught, 'is not registered.') < 0) then
		raise Error('Upgrading a library that is not registered did not raise 113242: ' + LCaught);

	DropLibrary('UpgradeUnregistered');
end;

// SaveUpgrade with an explicit version, and version order past revision nine. SaveUpgrade(name, version, script) writes the
// file and touches neither version number, which is how the IDE's upgrade editor saves an edit. This library's descriptor is
// 1.0.10 from the start, so the three upgrades saved below are all within the ceiling and the fourth, at 1.0.11, is above it.
// Order comes from UpgradeVersionsNode keying its result on Version, not from the directory listing: by file name
// Upgrade.1.0.10.d4 sorts before Upgrade.1.0.2.d4, so the trace would read 1;10;2; if the listing were what decided. The
// shipped libraries reach these revisions - Frontend's folder holds Upgrade.2.0.2.d4 and Upgrade.2.0.15.d4 - so this is the
// product's own case and not a contrived one.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeTeens', VersionNumber(1, 0, 10), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeTeens');

	SaveUpgrade('UpgradeTeens', VersionNumber(1, 0, 1), "create table TeensTrace { ID : Integer, Trace : String, key { ID } }; insert table { row { 0 ID, '1;' Trace } } into TeensTrace;");
	SaveUpgrade('UpgradeTeens', VersionNumber(1, 0, 2), "update TeensTrace set { Trace := Trace + '2;' };");
	SaveUpgrade('UpgradeTeens', VersionNumber(1, 0, 10), "update TeensTrace set { Trace := Trace + '10;' };");
	var LAboveCeiling : String := "update TeensTrace set { Trace := Trace + '11;' };";
	SaveUpgrade('UpgradeTeens', VersionNumber(1, 0, 11), LAboveCeiling);

	if (Version from row from (Libraries where Name = 'UpgradeTeens')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 10) then
		raise Error('SaveUpgrade with an explicit version changed the descriptor version.');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeTeens')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 10) then
		raise Error('SaveUpgrade with an explicit version changed the recorded version.');

	if (Count from row from (UpgradeVersions('UpgradeTeens') group add { Count() Count })) <> 4 then
		raise Error('UpgradeVersions did not list all four saved upgrades.');

	if LoadUpgrade('UpgradeTeens', VersionNumber(1, 0, 11)) <> LAboveCeiling then
		raise Error('LoadUpgrade did not return an upgrade saved above the descriptor version.');

	update LibraryVersions set { Version := VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) } where LibraryName = 'UpgradeTeens';
	UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeTeens');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeTeens')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 10) then
		raise Error('UpgradeLibrary did not stop at the descriptor version.');
end;

// 1;2;10; rather than 1;10;2;, and without the 11; of the upgrade saved above the descriptor version: the descriptor is the
// ceiling, which is what keeps an upgrade a release has not shipped yet from running.
if (Trace from row from .UpgradeTeens.TeensTrace) <> '1;2;10;' then
	raise Error('The upgrades did not run in version order, or the upgrade above the descriptor version ran: ' + (Trace from row from .UpgradeTeens.TeensTrace));

begin
	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeTeens');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeTeens');
end;

// A release may skip revisions. The descriptor is 1.0.5 and the only upgrade in the folder is 1.0.1: UpgradeLibraries runs that
// one and then records 1.0.5, because the recorded version is a statement about which files are applied and not about which
// upgrade scripts happened to exist. A reader who has not seen this reads it as a bug. UpgradeLibraries() is used here rather
// than UpgradeLibrary so that the operator which upgrades every registered library is covered doing real work as well as doing
// nothing.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeSkip', VersionNumber(1, 0, 5), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeSkip');
	SaveUpgrade('UpgradeSkip', VersionNumber(1, 0, 1), "create table SkipTrace { ID : Integer, Trace : String, key { ID } }; insert table { row { 0 ID, '1.0.1;' Trace } } into SkipTrace;");

	update LibraryVersions set { Version := VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) } where LibraryName = 'UpgradeSkip';
	UpgradeLibraries();

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeSkip')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 5) then
		raise Error('UpgradeLibraries did not record the descriptor version of a library whose upgrades stop short of it.');
end;

if (Trace from row from .UpgradeSkip.SkipTrace) <> '1.0.1;' then
	raise Error('The one upgrade below the descriptor version did not run exactly once: ' + (Trace from row from .UpgradeSkip.SkipTrace));

begin
	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeSkip');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeSkip');
end;

// Ordering through the operator a developer actually uses. Three upgrades injected one after another and applied by one
// UpgradeLibrary: each appends its own version to a string the first one creates, so the string pins both the order and that
// each script ran exactly once. Had 1.0.2 run first its table would not have existed and the failure would have been loud.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeOrder', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeOrder');

	var LInjected : VersionNumber := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeOrder', "create table OrderTrace { ID : Integer, Trace : String, key { ID } }; insert table { row { 0 ID, '1.0.1;' Trace } } into OrderTrace;");
	LInjected := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeOrder', "update OrderTrace set { Trace := Trace + '1.0.2;' };");
	LInjected := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeOrder', "update OrderTrace set { Trace := Trace + '1.0.3;' };");
	if LInjected <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 3) then
		raise Error('Three injects did not reach revision 3: ' + LInjected.ToString());

	update LibraryVersions set { Version := VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) } where LibraryName = 'UpgradeOrder';
	UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeOrder');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeOrder')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 3) then
		raise Error('UpgradeLibrary did not record the descriptor version after applying three upgrades.');
end;

if (Trace from row from .UpgradeOrder.OrderTrace) <> '1.0.1;1.0.2;1.0.3;' then
	raise Error('The injected upgrades did not run once each in version order: ' + (Trace from row from .UpgradeOrder.OrderTrace));

begin
	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeOrder');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeOrder');
end;

// Requisites first. UpgradeLibrary gathers the library's requisites transitively and upgrades them before the library itself,
// which is what lets an upgrade of a dependent library rely on what an upgrade of its requisite has just done.
// UpgradeDependent's upgrade names a table that UpgradeBase's upgrade creates, and can see it because it requires UpgradeBase.
// The requisite is pinned at 1.0 rather than at 1.0.0 because a reference that names a revision is not compatible with the
// revision bump that injecting into the required library makes, and the version change would be refused.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeBase', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeDependent', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, { LibraryReference('UpgradeBase', VersionNumber(1, 0)) }));
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeDependent');

	var LInjected : VersionNumber := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeBase', "create table BaseTrace { ID : Integer, Trace : String, key { ID } }; insert table { row { 0 ID, 'base;' Trace } } into BaseTrace;");
	LInjected := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeDependent', "update BaseTrace set { Trace := Trace + 'dependent;' };");

	update LibraryVersions set { Version := VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) } where LibraryName in { 'UpgradeBase', 'UpgradeDependent' };
	UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeDependent');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeBase')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('UpgradeLibrary did not upgrade the required library.');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeDependent')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('UpgradeLibrary did not upgrade the library it was given.');
end;

if (Trace from row from .UpgradeBase.BaseTrace) <> 'base;dependent;' then
	raise Error('The required library was not upgraded before the library that requires it: ' + (Trace from row from .UpgradeBase.BaseTrace));

begin
	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeDependent');
	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeBase');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeDependent');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeBase');
end;

// The two liberties the one shipped upgrade script takes, which nothing above covers. Frontend's Upgrades/Upgrade.2.0.8.d4
// opens with RollbackTransaction(), registers and upgrades another library, and closes with BeginTransaction(): it discards
// the transaction the upgrade machinery opened for it, works outside one, and hands a fresh one back. So an upgrade script
// may upgrade another library - UpgradeNested is deliberately not a requisite of UpgradeNesting, so the nested call is the
// only thing that can upgrade it, and the trace tells the two apart - and it may replace its own transaction. The closing
// BeginTransaction is not decoration: the machinery records the version and commits on the script's process after the script
// returns, so a script that leaves its transaction and does not open another fails on that commit with "No transaction in
// progress for this process" - measured by removing it, 2026-11-23. The opening RollbackTransaction is not what makes the
// nested call work, also measured: the nested call succeeds without it, because the transaction the machinery opens has
// written nothing to the catalog store yet and so holds no write lock on it.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeNested', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeNesting', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeNested');
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeNesting');

	var LInjected : VersionNumber := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeNested', "create table NestedTrace { ID : Integer, Trace : String, key { ID } }; insert table { row { 0 ID, 'nested;' Trace } } into NestedTrace;");
	LInjected := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeNesting', "RollbackTransaction(); UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeNested'); update .UpgradeNested.NestedTrace set { Trace := Trace + 'nesting;' }; BeginTransaction();");

	update LibraryVersions set { Version := VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) } where LibraryName in { 'UpgradeNested', 'UpgradeNesting' };
	UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeNesting');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeNested')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('An UpgradeLibrary call from inside an upgrade script did not upgrade the library it named.');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeNesting')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('An upgrade script that rolled back the transaction it was given and began another was not recorded as applied.');
end;

// The nested upgrade ran, and ran before the rest of the script that called it.
if (Trace from row from .UpgradeNested.NestedTrace) <> 'nested;nesting;' then
	raise Error('An upgrade script that upgrades another library did not see that library upgraded: ' + (Trace from row from .UpgradeNested.NestedTrace));

begin
	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeNesting');
	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeNested');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeNesting');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeNested');
end;

// A failing upgrade, and what it leaves behind. This is the case that matters most: an upgrade that stops halfway must leave a
// server that can be upgraded again once the broken script is fixed, not one that has to be rebuilt. The 1.0.2 upgrade raises
// on its first statement - deliberately before it changes anything, because whether a half-finished upgrade's own catalog
// changes roll back is a separate subject with tickets of its own - and the 1.0.3 upgrade after it must not run at all.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeFail', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeFail');

	var LInjected : VersionNumber := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeFail', "create table FailTrace { ID : Integer, Trace : String, key { ID } }; insert table { row { 0 ID, 'one;' Trace } } into FailTrace;");
	LInjected := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeFail', "raise Error('Deliberate failure from the 1.0.2 upgrade script.'); update FailTrace set { Trace := Trace + 'two;' };");
	LInjected := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeFail', "update FailTrace set { Trace := Trace + 'three;' };");

	update LibraryVersions set { Version := VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) } where LibraryName = 'UpgradeFail';

	var LCaught : String := '';
	try
		UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeFail');
	except on E : Error do
		LCaught := E.GetDetailedErrorDescription();
	end;
	if IndexOf(LCaught, 'Deliberate failure from the 1.0.2 upgrade script.') < 0 then
		raise Error('A failing upgrade script did not carry its own error out of UpgradeLibrary: ' + LCaught);

	// The recorded version is the last version that actually applied - neither the descriptor version nor the version the run
	// started from. That is what lets the next run resume rather than start over or skip what it has not applied.
	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeFail')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 1) then
		raise Error('A failed upgrade run did not leave the recorded version at the last upgrade that applied.');

	if not exists (LoadedLibraries where Name = 'UpgradeFail') then
		raise Error('A failed upgrade run left the library unregistered.');
end;

// The first upgrade's row, and nothing from the two after it.
if (Trace from row from .UpgradeFail.FailTrace) <> 'one;' then
	raise Error('A failed upgrade run ran more than the upgrades before the failure: ' + (Trace from row from .UpgradeFail.FailTrace));

// Deleting the broken upgrade and running again resumes from 1.0.1, which is what proves the recorded version was left usable
// rather than merely unchanged. It is also the only meaningful cover for DeleteUpgrade.
begin
	DeleteUpgrade('UpgradeFail', VersionNumber(1, 0, 2));

	if exists (UpgradeVersions('UpgradeFail') where Version = VersionNumber(1, 0, 2)) then
		raise Error('DeleteUpgrade left the upgrade in the folder.');

	UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeFail');

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeFail')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 3) then
		raise Error('The upgrade run after the broken upgrade had been deleted did not finish.');

	if (Trace from row from .UpgradeFail.FailTrace) <> 'one;three;' then
		raise Error('The resumed upgrade run did not apply exactly the upgrade left after the failure: ' + (Trace from row from .UpgradeFail.FailTrace));

	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeFail');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeFail');
end;

// UpgradeLibraries() across several libraries, and what it leaves behind when one of them fails. It visits every registered
// library in requisite-first order and has no catch around that loop, so the first failure ends the whole run: every library
// after the failing one is left un-upgraded at its own recorded version, and the error names only the library that failed. An
// administrator who upgrades a server after a release therefore has to read the recorded versions to learn what applied and
// what did not. The two libraries here require nothing of each other, so the order is the order they were registered in,
// which is what makes "the one after it" mean anything; and the second library's upgrade would create a table, so the absence
// of that table is independent evidence that it never ran.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeStopsFirst', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeStopsSecond', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeStopsFirst');
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeStopsSecond');

	var LInjected : VersionNumber := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeStopsFirst', "raise Error('Deliberate failure from the first of two pending libraries.');");
	LInjected := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeStopsSecond', "create table StopsTrace { ID : Integer, Trace : String, key { ID } }; insert table { row { 0 ID, 'second;' Trace } } into StopsTrace;");

	// Stands for: the files of version 1.0.1 of both libraries have arrived and the server has been restarted on them.
	update LibraryVersions set { Version := VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) } where LibraryName in { 'UpgradeStopsFirst', 'UpgradeStopsSecond' };

	var LCaught : String := '';
	try
		UpgradeLibraries();
	except on E : Error do
		LCaught := E.GetDetailedErrorDescription();
	end;
	if IndexOf(LCaught, 'Deliberate failure from the first of two pending libraries.') < 0 then
		raise Error('UpgradeLibraries did not surface the error the failing upgrade script raised: ' + LCaught);

	if IndexOf(LCaught, 'UpgradeStopsSecond') >= 0 then
		raise Error('The error named the library that was never reached: ' + LCaught);

	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeStopsFirst')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) then
		raise Error('The library whose upgrade failed did not stay at the version the run started from.');

	// The library after the failing one is the point of this section: nothing upgraded it and nothing said so.
	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeStopsSecond')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) then
		raise Error('The library after the failing one was upgraded anyway.');

	if exists (CatalogObjects where Name = 'UpgradeStopsSecond.StopsTrace') then
		raise Error('The upgrade of the library after the failing one ran.');

	if not (UpgradeRequired from row from (LibraryView where Name = 'UpgradeStopsSecond')) then
		raise Error('The library after the failing one stopped reporting an upgrade as required.');

	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeStopsSecond');
	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeStopsFirst');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeStopsSecond');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeStopsFirst');
end;

// The one caller the DropLibrary refusal takes away, pinned so that it stays a decision rather than a surprise: an upgrade script
// runs inside the transaction the machinery opened for it (the BeginTransaction() in InternalUpgradeLibrary's statement), which is
// a transaction that can roll back after the library's directory has already been deleted, so a DropLibrary there is refused with
// 113251. No shipped upgrade script drops a library and none may; docs/migration-notes.md carries this as a change a customer's
// upgrade script could notice. The library the script tries to drop is left unregistered, so that nothing but the refusal stops it.
begin
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeDropVictim', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	CreateLibrary(LibraryDescriptor('UpgradeDropper', VersionNumber(1, 0, 0), "", list(FileReference){}, list(LibraryReference){}));
	RegisterLibrary('UpgradeDropper');

	var LInjected : VersionNumber := InjectUpgrade('UpgradeDropper', "DropLibrary('UpgradeDropVictim');");
	update LibraryVersions set { Version := VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) } where LibraryName = 'UpgradeDropper';

	var LCaught : String := '';
	try
		UpgradeLibrary('UpgradeDropper');
	except on E : Error do
		LCaught := E.GetDetailedErrorDescription();
	end;
	if (IndexOf(LCaught, ':113251 --->') < 0) or (IndexOf(LCaught, 'cannot be dropped inside an explicit transaction') < 0) then
		raise Error('DropLibrary inside an upgrade script was not refused with 113251: ' + LCaught);

	if not exists (Libraries where Name = 'UpgradeDropVictim') then
		raise Error('The refused drop inside an upgrade script removed the library anyway.');

	// The refused upgrade leaves the recorded version where the failing-upgrade section above leaves it: at the last version that
	// applied, which here is the version the run started from.
	if (Version from row from (LibraryVersions where LibraryName = 'UpgradeDropper')) <> VersionNumber(1, 0, 0) then
		raise Error('The refused upgrade did not leave the recorded version at the version the run started from.');

	UnregisterLibrary('UpgradeDropper');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeDropper');
	DropLibrary('UpgradeDropVictim');
end;

if exists (Libraries where Name in { 'UpgradeArith', 'UpgradeNoRevision', 'UpgradeBadVersion', 'UpgradeUnregistered', 'UpgradeTeens', 'UpgradeSkip', 'UpgradeOrder', 'UpgradeBase', 'UpgradeDependent', 'UpgradeNested', 'UpgradeNesting', 'UpgradeFail', 'UpgradeStopsFirst', 'UpgradeStopsSecond', 'UpgradeDropper', 'UpgradeDropVictim' }) then
	raise Error('The script left a library behind.');

if exists (LibraryVersions where LibraryName in { 'UpgradeArith', 'UpgradeTeens', 'UpgradeSkip', 'UpgradeOrder', 'UpgradeBase', 'UpgradeDependent', 'UpgradeNested', 'UpgradeNesting', 'UpgradeFail', 'UpgradeStopsFirst', 'UpgradeStopsSecond', 'UpgradeDropper' }) then
	raise Error('The script left a library version behind.');
